From: Recalbox <contact@recalbox.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] lightgun: validate the calibration by shooting, detect gun ports

Three problems, one calibration flow.

AUX_B used to skip (phase 1) or validate (phase 2). But lightgun.xml binds it,
for the SuperScope "turbo" games, to the very same physical input as the
trigger (trigger_mbtn = aux_b_mbtn = 1), so the first shot at the first target
ended the calibration. AUX_B is no longer intercepted and reaches the core.

No button can replace it: on the gun's port only its three mouse buttons are
readable. The joypad-only binds (START, SELECT, AUX_C, D-pad) are dead, since
that player's joypad index does not point at the gun and udev reads them with
joypad->button(joyport_of_that_player, joykey). So the calibration is now
validated by shooting: phase 1 is mandatory, and phase 2 draws a centre
bullseye, "SHOOT HERE TO START". AUX_A and START (the GunCon B, bound through
gun_start_mbtn) both mean "retry", from the test phase only. START is honoured
solely from the deliberate force-poll inside the TRIGGER read: RetroArch also
reads it for hotkey handling, outside the core's update_input, and those stray
reads would restart the calibration on their own.

Finally, the overlay was gated on the configured libretro device masked with
RETRO_DEVICE_MASK, which misses fceumm: its Zapper is a MOUSE subclass (258),
so Duck Hunt drew no targets and, the trigger staying masked while
calibrating, saw no shots at all. Key it instead on what the core actually
does -- it reads the port with RETRO_DEVICE_LIGHTGUN ids -- and require a gun
to be configured there: on 2-player games the core polls every port as a
lightgun even with a single gun plugged (flycast, House of the Dead), which
resurrected a phantom player 2, re-opened the calibration and broke the target
colours. configgen zeroes the device of gunless ports.
